  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Logan Webb pitched like a winner. The Giants couldn't give him one.",
    "what_this_means": "Webb allowed two runs over eight innings. A quality start that should have been enough in any tight game. But a four-hit offense, six strikeouts, and a single run left him stranded. Casey Schmitt drove in the only run, a lone RBI that never became part of something larger. This is the central frustration: precision without reward. The pitching held. The bats held nothing. One converted RISP opportunity doesn't erase the reality that runners on second and third in the third inning produced just that single run. Miami scored its two and never let go. The Giants were chasing by the second inning and never recovered from that moment. Webb did everything asked. The rest couldn't answer.",
    "what_to_watch": "This team is inconsistent in the way that ends seasons. They have the pieces to beat anybody on a given day. Webb proved that Friday. But they can't sustain the alignment required to actually win those games regularly. Good pitching nights have become a curse rather than a gift because the offense isn't built to take advantage of them. Four hits against Miami's starter was invitation enough. They didn't accept. The margin is paper-thin now: eighteen games back, losing streaks hitting three, and the ability to lose winnable games piling up. This isn't a slump where patience fixes it. This is a structural mismatch between what the pitching can hold and what the offense can produce. They're 0.500 against mediocre arms because they can't manufacture runs from quality starts. That imbalance gets harder to ignore.",
